description:
Write the kind of library almost every C program ends up with: a logger with levels, a printf-like interface, an in-memory history ring, and safe use from several threads.

```c
struct logger *log = logger_new(LOG_INFO, 4);       /* keep the last 4 lines */
LOG(log, LOG_DEBUG, "not shown: %d", 1);
LOG(log, LOG_WARN, "disk %d%% full", 91);
LOG(log, LOG_ERROR, "cannot open %s", "save.dat");
logger_history(log, buf, sizeof buf);
/* "[WARN] disk 91% full\n[ERROR] cannot open save.dat\n" */
logger_free(log);
```

```c
enum log_level { LOG_DEBUG, LOG_INFO, LOG_WARN, LOG_ERROR };

struct logger;                                                     /* opaque */
struct logger *logger_new(enum log_level min_level, int history);  /* history: lines kept, 1 to 64 */
void logger_free(struct logger *log);
int logger_write(struct logger *log, enum log_level level, const char *format, ...);
int logger_count(struct logger *log, enum log_level level);        /* lines logged at that level */
size_t logger_history(struct logger *log, char *out, size_t size); /* oldest first, like snprintf */

#define LOG(log, level, ...) logger_write((log), (level), __VA_ARGS__)
```

- `logger_write` ignores messages below the minimum level (returning 0). Otherwise it formats the message with `vsnprintf` (cut to 120 characters), stores `"[LEVEL] message"` in the history ring (the oldest line is dropped when it is full), counts it, and returns 1.
- `logger_history` writes the stored lines, oldest first, each followed by `"\n"`, into `out`; like `snprintf` it never overflows, always ends with a zero (when `size > 0`) and returns the length the full text needs.
- Level names: `DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARN`, `ERROR`.
- Every function is safe to call from several threads at once (an `mtx_t` in the logger).
- `logger_new` returns `NULL` for a history outside 1 to 64 or when out of memory.

